        'A Weibull plot starts with the failure ages sorted from short to long. Each failure gets a median rank, the best estimate of the share of units failed by that age. Bernard’s approximation: (i − 0.3) ÷ (n + 0.4), where i is the order number and n the sample size. With five failures, the first gets 0.7 ÷ 5.4 = 13%.\n\nOn Weibull paper the axes are scaled so a Weibull is a straight line. The slope is beta; the age where the line crosses 63.2% is eta.',
        'Suspensions, units still running or removed without failing, change the ranks: they push the ranks of later failures down. Hand methods adjust the ranks (Johnson’s method); most software uses maximum likelihood estimation (MLE), which finds the beta and eta that make the observed data most likely.\n\nAbernethy’s New Weibull Handbook prefers rank regression for small samples and MLE when there are many suspensions. Analysts argue about it; ask which method was used.',
        'For decisions, a B-life is often more useful than eta. The B10 life is the age by which 10% of units have failed: t = eta × (−ln 0.9)^(1/beta), and −ln 0.9 = 0.105. With eta 20,000 hours and beta 2: 20,000 × 0.105^0.5 ≈ 6,490 hours.\n\nBearing makers quote L10 life the same way. Use a B-life to set a replacement age or a warranty, not eta: by eta, almost two in three units have already gone.',
        'Every fit has uncertainty, shown as a confidence interval: the range the true value probably lies in. With five failures, a fitted beta of 2 could plausibly be anywhere from about 1 to 4. Report beta with its interval.\n\nCurved plots warn you. Points bending at the start can mean a failure-free period at the beginning, handled with a three-parameter Weibull, or mixed failure modes. A dog-leg, two straight lines with a kink, means two modes. Fix the data before reaching for a fancier model.',
        'Weibull describes the life of one part that is replaced by a new one. A whole machine that is repaired again and again is a repairable system: then look at whether the time between failures shrinks or grows over calendar time.\n\nA Crow-AMSAA plot draws cumulative failures against cumulative time on log-log axes: a slope below 1 means improving, above 1 getting worse. It answers “is our reliability programme working?”. The sharp question: “Is this a part life or a system trend, and what is the interval on beta?”',
